<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Eldiguzids</h1><p class="st-meta">1136 CE – 1225 CE · capital Nakhchivan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Turkic dynasty of atabegs who, from Nakhchivan and Tabriz, controlled the late Seljuk sultans and dominated north-western Iran and Arran.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shams-al-din-eldiguz">Shams al-Din Eldiguz</a>, a former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kipchaks">Kipchak</a> slave, rose in the service of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> and around 1136 was made governor of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/caucasian-albania">Arran</a> and atabeg, or guardian, of the prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arslan-shah-ibn-toghrul">Arslan Shah</a>. After placing Arslan Shah on the throne of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/seljuk-sultanate-of-iraq">Seljuk Sultanate of Iraq</a> in 1160, he became the real power behind it.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The atabegs ruled <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/caucasian-albania">Arran</a> and much of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhchivan">Nakhchivan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a>, making and unmaking sultans. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahan-pahlavan">Jahan Pahlavan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizil-arslan">Qizil Arslan</a> continued their father's dominance. Their court patronised Persian poetry, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizami-ganjavi">Nizami Ganjavi</a>, and the architect Ajami Nakhchivani built the Momine Khatun mausoleum in Nakhchivan in 1186.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-georgia">Kingdom of Georgia</a> defeated the atabeg <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nusrat-al-din-abu-bakr">Abu Bakr</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-shamkor">Shamkor</a> in 1195, and Mongol raids further weakened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a>, the last ruler. In 1225 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalal-al-din-mangburni">Jalal al-Din</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmian Empire</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>, ending the dynasty.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/eldiguzids.jpg" alt="Eldiguzids" width="320" height="276"><figcaption>Image: Demis MAP server · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Eldiguzids_in_1180_CE.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1136 CE – 1225 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nakhchivan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Eldiguz, Jahan Pahlavan, Qizil Arslan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Persian; Turkic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Atabegs of Azerbaijan, Ildeguzids, Ildenizids, Eldiguzids, Ildegizids, Eldiguzid dynasty</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1180&amp;focus=eldiguzids" aria-label="Eldiguzids on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5804 772 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Eldiguzids lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6124 1028L6148 1029M6124 1028L6481 1127M6124 1028L6480 1127M6124 1028L6231 1174"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6148 1029h0M6481 1127h0M6480 1127h0M6231 1174h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6124 1028h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6124 1028h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Nakhchivan. <a href="/atlas?year=1180&amp;focus=eldiguzids">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

